\section{Authentication}{

A NASA Earthdata bearer token is required for any read that hits a
DAAC endpoint. spacelaser reads it from the \code{EARTHDATA_TOKEN}
environment variable and sends it as \code{Authorization: Bearer} to the
DAAC. Set it in \verb{~/.Renviron} as \verb{EARTHDATA_TOKEN=<token>}. Tokens
expire after 60 days; an expired token surfaces a clear error at read
time.
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=generate_ed_token]{generate_ed_token()}} --- mint (or reuse) an Earthdata token from
your username and password, optionally persisting it to
\verb{~/.Renviron}
}
}

\section{Options}{

\code{spacelaser.cmr_max_seconds}: total time, in seconds, that a request to
NASA CMR or the Earthdata token API keeps retrying after timeouts,
connection failures and HTTP 429, 500, 502, 503 or 504. Default \code{600}.
Retries back off exponentially, at most 60 s apart; \code{0} disables them.
Token creation is never retried. A transient failure that remains raises
an error of class \code{spacelaser_upstream_error}, with fields \code{service} (\code{"cmr"} or
\code{"earthdata"}), \code{url} and \code{status} (\code{NA} for a connection failure).
A CMR search also retries an HTTP 400 twice, 2 s and then 5 s apart,
since CMR has returned spurious 400s during incidents; a 400 that
persists stays an unclassed error with \code{status} 400. A failed CMR
request carries \code{service}, \code{url}, \code{status} and \code{cmr_errors}, the error
text of CMR's response (\code{character()} when there is none), which is
also shown in the message.
}

